### Governance Oversight and Accountability

Unilever’s Board holds ultimate accountability for the management of all material impacts, risks, and opportunities (IROs), including sustainability-related matters, and delegates day-to-day oversight to the Unilever Leadership Executive (ULE) [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_7]. The Board’s governance structure is supported by four key subcommittees: the Corporate Responsibility Committee (CRC), Audit Committee, Remuneration Committee, and Nominating and Corporate Governance Committee — each with terms of reference published on Unilever’s website at unilever.com [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_7]. The CRC reviews Unilever’s sustainability strategy, tracks progress against sustainability goals, and assesses the reputational impact of material IROs, receiving updates from the global Sustainability function five times per year [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_7]. The Audit Committee oversees the effectiveness of risk management processes—including the double materiality assessment (DMA)—non-financial disclosures in the Annual Report and Accounts (including under the ESRS), and assurance activities over those disclosures [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_7]. The Remuneration Committee aligns the long-term Performance Share Plan (PSP) with sustainability priorities, assigning 15% weight to the Sustainability Progress Index (SPI) in PSP awards for 2025 [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_8][unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_9]. The Nominating and Corporate Governance Committee ensures the Board includes members with relevant sustainability expertise [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_7]. GRI 2-11 (Board sustainability expertise) is not quantified in the source material; no figure is provided for the number or percentage of Board directors with documented sustainability or ESG governance credentials [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_7].

### GRI 205-1: Anti-Corruption Policy and Scope

Unilever maintains a zero-tolerance policy toward corruption and bribery, as codified in its Code of Business Principles (COBP) and supporting Code Policies, which prohibit public and commercial bribery—including facilitation payments—regardless of financial value [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_164]. These policies apply to all employees and extend to business partners through the Responsible Partner Policy (RPP), which mandates adherence to Unilever’s anti-corruption standards across the value chain [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_164][unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_161]. The RPP applies beyond Tier 1 suppliers, expecting cascading of equivalent requirements into their supply chains [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_161]. No baseline, target, or timeline is disclosed for RPP requirement cascade compliance across supply chain tiers [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_161].

### GRI 205-2: Anti-Corruption Training

All employees completed mandatory annual anti-corruption and anti-bribery training in 2025; Unilever Board members also received training on this subject [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_165]. The training content is based on learnings from investigations, risk assessments, and business partnering, and additional bespoke training is offered for employees in external-facing commercial roles who face greater corruption or bribery risk [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_165]. No absolute number, completion rate (%), or comparator for 2024 is disclosed for employee or supplier training [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_165]. Unilever does not report the number or percentage of Tier 1 suppliers completing anti-corruption training in 2025 or 2024 [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_165].

### GRI 205-3: Whistleblower Reports and Resolution Status

The context does not disclose any quantitative data on whistleblower reports — including volume, type, resolution status, or timeframes — for 2023, 2024, or 2025. While reporting channels are described (e.g., Speak Up platform and hotline), no metrics on usage, substantiation rates, or pending cases are provided [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_160]. Therefore, GRI 205-3 metrics (number of reports, substantiated/unsubstantiated counts, average resolution time) are not covered in source material.

### GRI 205-4: Investigations, Outcomes, and Remedial Actions

The context states that “all potential material cases of corruption and bribery related to public officials are reported to our Chief Legal Officer and Chief Business Integrity Officer, who oversee investigations”, and that “completed reports are reviewed and approved by the Global Head of Investigations” [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_164][unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_161]. It further notes that Business Integrity Committees are responsible for ensuring timely investigation of all potential Code breaches raised within 60 days, though final determination may take longer depending on complexity [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_161]. However, no figures are provided for the number of substantiated corruption allegations, average investigation resolution time, or remedial actions taken in 2023–2025 [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_161]. No disclosure is made of near-misses, operational challenges in high-risk regions, or enforcement difficulties in lower-tier supplier relationships [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_161].

### TCFD Governance A1–A3: Climate Risk Integration into Board Oversight

The context confirms that climate- and nature-related risks are assessed through scenario analysis aligned with TCFD and TNFD frameworks, and that insights inform medium- and long-term strategic planning [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_37][unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_30]. The Audit Committee oversees the double materiality assessment (DMA), which includes climate-related risks, and the CRC reviews reputational impacts of material IROs [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_7]. However, the source material does not specify how climate risks are integrated into Board agendas, nor does it disclose whether climate competence is reflected in director skill matrices or board evaluation outcomes [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_7]. TCFD Governance A2 (integration of climate risk into board oversight) is therefore not quantitatively or procedurally detailed.

### Sustainability Performance Linkage and Metrics

In 2025, the SPI was determined by assessing performance against four sustainability targets related to climate, nature, plastics, and livelihoods [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_9]. The SPI accounts for 15% of the total PSP award and is assessed jointly by the CRC and Remuneration Committee [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_9]. However, the source material provides neither the 2024 baseline value nor the 2025 actual score of the SPI, nor its calculation methodology [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_9]. Page 97 is referenced for “SPI outcomes for 2025”, but no numerical value is extracted from the context [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_9].

### Political Engagement and Supplier Governance

Unilever engages in advocacy and lobbying—directly and indirectly through trade associations and industry groups—to advance policy in climate, nature, plastics, and livelihoods, always aligned with its Code and Code Policies requiring honesty, integrity, openness, and compliance with applicable laws [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_165]. Oversight of national government engagement and lobbying activity in 2025 was led by the Chief Corporate Affairs and Communications Officer [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_165]. Supplier relationships are governed by the Responsible Sourcing and Business Partnering Code Policy, mandating fair treatment and requiring third parties to uphold Unilever’s standards—including anti-corruption requirements—through onboarding, contracting, and ongoing monitoring [unilever-sustainability-statement_chunk_161].

### Not covered in source material

The provided context does not disclose:  
(1) The number or percentage of Board directors with formal sustainability or ESG governance credentials (GRI 2-11);  
(2) The absolute number or completion rate (%) of employees and Tier 1 suppliers completing mandatory anti-corruption training in 2025 (GRI 205-2);  
(3) The 2024 baseline value and 2025 actual score of the Sustainability Progress Index (SPI), or its calculation methodology (GRI 2-21, TCFD Governance A3);  
(4) The number of whistleblower reports, their substantiation status, resolution timelines, or volume per year (GRI 205-3);  
(5) The number of substantiated corruption allegations, average investigation resolution time, or remedial actions taken (GRI 205-4);  
(6) A quantified anti-corruption target (e.g., reduction in unassessed high-risk suppliers) with 2024 baseline and deadline (GRI 205-1);  
(7) A phased timeline or baseline metric for RPP requirement cascade beyond Tier 1 suppliers (GRI 205-1);  
(8) Operational challenges encountered in enforcing anti-corruption standards in specific high-risk regions or supplier tiers (GRI 205-4);  
(9) Whether any 2025 PSP awards were reduced due to unmet SPI thresholds (GRI 2-21);  
(10) The name or scope of the third-party assurance provider covering anti-corruption policies and governance disclosures (GRI 205-1);  
(11) How climate and nature risks are specifically integrated into Board agendas or assessed against director skill matrices (TCFD Governance A2);  
(12) Board evaluation outcomes related to sustainability competence (GRI 2-11);  
(13) Quantitative metrics on whistleblower reports (volume, type, resolution status) per GRI 205-3;  
(14) Investigations into alleged corruption, including outcomes and remedial actions per GRI 205-4;  
(15) The scope and limitations of third-party assurance over anti-corruption governance disclosures (GRI 205-1);  
(16) The percentage of Tier 1 suppliers subject to anti-corruption due diligence in 2025, with a 2024 comparator (GRI 205-2);  
(17) The number of unsubstantiated or pending whistleblower reports and associated governance implications (GRI 205-3);  
(18) A measurable anti-corruption target with baseline year, deadline, and unit (e.g., reduction in high-risk jurisdictions) (GRI 205-1).
